{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["<b>And he saw Amalek</b> – He contemplated the calamity of Amalek and said: \"The first of the nations is Amalek\" — he preceded all of them to wage war against Israel. Thus Onkelos rendered (Onkelos on Numbers 24:20), and his end shall be to perish by your hand — \"you shall blot out the remembrance of Amalek\" (Deuteronomy 25:19); this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Numbers 24:20). And it is possible that \"and he saw\" is according to its plain sense, that he was at the top of Peor overlooking the face of the wilderness, and he looked toward the land of Amalek, who dwelt on that mountain. And he said that now he is the first of the nations, for he is reckoned at the head of the peoples, for they were mighty men and men of valor in battle. Were it not so, it would not have entered his mind to come to wage war against Israel, nor would Moses have needed to choose men and to pray and to lift up his hands because of his weakness, and build an altar and call its name HaShem Nissi (Exodus 17:15), for he considered it a great sign. And therefore he said this — that he is the first of the peoples, yet his end shall perish more than all of them, as it says: \"For I will utterly blot out the remembrance of Amalek from under the heavens\" (ibid. verse 14). And likewise \"the first of the oils\" (Amos 6:6) — the choicest among them, which is counted first. And likewise \"the first of the devoted things\" (I Samuel 15:21), \"for a head and for a captain\" (Judges 11:11), \"chief spices\" (Exodus 30:23)."], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:08:36.786889589Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}